QAC_021,Procedural,Hard,Administrative,How is the Constitution of Sri Lanka amended?,"Article 82 of the Constitution provides that no Bill for the amendment of any provision of the Constitution shall be placed on the Order Paper of Parliament, unless the provision to be repealed, altered or added, and consequential amendments, if any, are expressly specified in the Bill and it is described in the long title thereof as being an Act for the amendment of the Constitution.",Article 82,"Art. 82: (1) No Bill for the amendment of any provision Amendment or of the Constitution shall be placed on the Order Paper of repeal of the Constitution Parliament, unless the provision to be repealed, altere",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
QAC_022,Factual,Easy,Administrative,What is the National Day and National Anthem of Sri Lanka under the Constitution?,"Article 8 of the Constitution provides that the National Day of the Republic of Sri Lanka shall be the fourth day of February. Article 7 provides that the National Anthem of the Republic of Sri Lanka shall be Sri Lanka Matha, the words and music of which are set out in the Third Schedule to the Constitution.","Article 8, Article 7","Art. 8: The National Day of the Republic of Sri Lanka Day shall be the fourth day of February 1 - Substituted by the Seventh Amendment to the Constitution Sec. 2(a). 2 - Substituted by the Seventh Amendment t || Art. 7: The National Anthem of the Republic of Sri Anthem Lanka shall be Sri Lanka Matha, the words and music of which are set out in the Third Schedule. The National",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
QAC_023,Cross_referenced,Hard,Administrative,How does Article 9 on Buddhism relate to the right to freedom of religion under Article 10?,"Article 9 gives Buddhism the foremost place and imposes a duty on the State to protect and foster the Buddha Sasana. However, Article 9 itself qualifies this by stating it is done 'while assuring to all religions the rights granted by Articles 10 and 14(1)(e)'. Article 10 guarantees every person freedom of thought, conscience and religion, including the freedom to have or adopt a religion of their choice. Therefore, the constitutional primacy of Buddhism under Article 9 does not override the individual right to freedom of religion guaranteed under Article 10.","Article 9, Article 10","Art. 9: The Republic of Sri Lanka shall give to Buddhism Buddhism the foremost place and accordingly it shall be the duty of the State to protect and foster the Buddha Sasana, while assuring to all religions || Art. 10: Every person is entitled to freedom of thought, thought, conscience and religion, including the freedom to have or conscience and religion to adopt a religion or belief of his choice. Freedom",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
QAC_024,Interpretive,Hard,Administrative,Do the fundamental duties under Article 28 limit the exercise of fundamental rights?,"Article 28 of the Constitution provides that the exercise and enjoyment of rights and freedoms are inseparable from the performance of duties and obligations. The duties listed include upholding the Constitution and law, furthering national interest, and working conscientiously. However, Article 29 makes clear that the provisions of Chapter VI (which includes Article 28) do not confer or impose legally enforceable rights or obligations. Therefore, while Article 28 links rights and duties conceptually, it does not operate as a legal restriction that courts can use to limit the exercise of fundamental rights guaranteed in Chapter III.","Article 28, Article 29",Art. 28: The exercise and enjoyment of rights and Fundamental freedoms are inseparable from the performance of duties duties and obligations and accordingly it is the duty of every person in Sri Lanka  (a) to || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame,LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
QAC_025,Factual,Medium,Criminal,Does the Constitution protect against retrospective criminal penalties?,"Yes. Article 13(6) of the Constitution provides that no person shall be held guilty of an offence on account of any act or omission which did not, at the time of such act or omission, constitute such an offence, and no penalty shall be imposed for any offence more severe than the penalty applicable at the time the offence was committed. This prohibition on retrospective penal legislation is a fundamental right under Chapter III.",Article 13,"Art. 13: (1) No person shall be arrested except according Freedom from to procedure established by law. Any person arrested shall arbitrary arrest, be informed of the reason for his arrest. detention and punis",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
QAC_026,Factual,Medium,Administrative,What does the Constitution say about Sri Lanka's obligation to ensure social security?,"Article 27(9) of the Constitution provides that the State shall ensure social security and welfare. Article 27(2) further pledges the State to promote the welfare of the People by securing and protecting a social order in which justice shall guide all institutions of national life. These are Directive Principles of State Policy under Chapter VI, meaning they guide governance but are not directly enforceable in courts.","Article 27, Article 27","Art. 27: (3) The State shall safeguard the independence, sovereignty, unity and the territorial integrity of Sri Lanka. (4) The State shall strengthen and broaden the democratic structure of government and the || Art. 27: (3) The State shall safeguard the independence, sovereignty, unity and the territorial integrity of Sri Lanka. (4) The State shall strengthen and broaden the democratic structure of government and the",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-03-08,,,,,,,
