QAC_046,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Who presides over Parliament and how is the Speaker elected?,"Article 64(1) of the Constitution provides that Parliament shall, at its first meeting after a general election, elect from among its members a Speaker. The Speaker shall preside over Parliament. Article 65 provides for a Deputy Speaker who presides in the absence of the Speaker.",Article 64,"Art. 64: (1) Parliament shall, at its first meeting after a Speaker, Deputy General Election, elect three Members to be respectively Speaker and the Speaker, the Deputy Speaker and Chairman of Committees Deput",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
QAC_047,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,How often must Parliament meet and what constitutes a quorum?,"Article 73 provides that the quorum for a sitting of Parliament is twenty members. If at any time during a meeting of Parliament the attention of the person presiding is drawn to the fact that fewer than twenty members are present, the person presiding shall adjourn the sitting without question put. The Constitution corpus does not contain a provision prescribing how often Parliament must meet per year in the passages assessed; Article 71 deals with adjournment rather than with a mandatory annual-session requirement.",Article 73,"Art. 73: If at any time during a meeting of Parliament Quorum the attention of the person presiding is drawn to the fact that there are fewer than twenty Members present, the person presiding shall, subject to",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
QAC_048,Procedural,Hard,Constitutional,Under what conditions may the President dissolve Parliament?,"Article 70 of the Constitution gives the President power to dissolve Parliament. However, the President shall not dissolve Parliament until the expiration of not less than two years and six months from the date appointed for the first meeting of Parliament, unless Parliament itself requests dissolution by resolution. There are also restrictions on dissolution after a no-confidence motion has been received by the Speaker.",Article 70,"Art. 70: 53[(1) The President may, from time to time, by Parliament Proclamation summon, prorogue and dissolve Parliament: Provided that  (a) subject to the provisions of sub-paragraph (d), the President shall",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
QAC_049,Procedural,Medium,Constitutional,What is the procedure for passing a Bill in Parliament?,"Article 79 of the Constitution requires the Speaker to endorse on every Bill passed by Parliament a certificate that the Bill has been duly passed. Under Article 80, a Bill passed by Parliament shall become law when the Speaker's certificate is endorsed thereon. Where the Cabinet of Ministers certifies that a Bill is to be submitted for approval by the People at a Referendum, the Bill shall not become law unless approved at such Referendum.","Article 79, Article 80",Art. 79: The Speaker shall endorse on every Bill passed Certificate of Speaker by Parliament a certificate in the following form: This Bill (here state the short title of the Bill) has been duly passed by Parl,LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
QAC_050,Procedural,Hard,Constitutional,How is the Constitution amended and what special requirements apply to certain provisions?,"Article 82 provides the general procedure: a Bill for amendment requires a two-thirds majority of all members of Parliament. Article 83 lists entrenched provisions — including those relating to sovereignty, unitary status, Buddhism (Article 9), and fundamental rights (Articles 10-14) — which additionally require approval by the People at a Referendum after passing with a two-thirds majority in Parliament.",Article 82,"Art. 82: (1) No Bill for the amendment of any provision Amendment or of the Constitution shall be placed on the Order Paper of repeal of the Constitution Parliament, unless the provision to be repealed, altere",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
