QAC_056,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What is the Consolidated Fund and how are withdrawals controlled?,"Article 148 of the Constitution establishes Parliament's control over public finance: all revenues, grants and other moneys shall form the Consolidated Fund. Article 150 provides that no sum shall be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under the authority of a warrant signed by the Minister of Finance, and only where Parliament has appropriated the sum for specified public services.",Article 148,"Art. 148: Parliament shall have full control over public Control of finance. No tax, rate or any other levy shall be imposed by Parliament over public any local authority or any other public authority, except f",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
QAC_057,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What is the Appropriation Bill and when must it be presented to Parliament?,"Under Article 152 of the Constitution, no Bill or motion authorizing the disposal of funds from the Consolidated Fund or the imposition, repeal, or reduction of any tax may be introduced in Parliament except by a Minister, and only if the Bill has been approved by the Cabinet of Ministers. The Appropriation Bill, which authorizes annual government expenditure, must accordingly be presented by the Minister in charge of Finance. Parliament must approve the Appropriation Bill before funds can be drawn from the Consolidated Fund under Articles 149 and 150.",Article 152,"Art. 152: No Bill or motion, authorizing the disposal of, or provisions the imposition of charges upon, the Consolidated Fund or a",LEGAL_EXPERT_1 (Human-Annotated),2026-05-04,,,,,,,
