QAC_251,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Does the Constitution recognise a right to return to Sri Lanka?,Article 14(1)(i) provides that every citizen is entitled to the freedom to return to Sri Lanka.,Article 14,"Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_252,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can the State restrict public assemblies in the interests of racial harmony?,Article 15(3) provides that the exercise and operation of the fundamental right to freedom of peaceful assembly (Article 14(1)(b)) shall be subject to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law in the interests of racial and religious harmony only.,"Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_253,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,A journalist is prosecuted for criticism of Parliament. Which constitutional restrictions on freedom of speech may be relevant?,"Article 14(1)(a) guarantees freedom of speech and expression including publication. Article 15(2) permits restrictions on this freedom prescribed by law in the interests of racial and religious harmony, national security, public order, protection of public health or morality, parliamentary privilege, contempt of court, or for the purpose of securing recognition and respect for the rights and freedoms of others. A prosecution for parliamentary privilege-related speech would need to be assessed against these grounds.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_254,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Does the Constitution protect the freedom to conduct a business or trade?,"Article 14(1)(g) provides that every citizen is entitled to the freedom to engage in any lawful occupation, profession, trade, business or enterprise. This freedom is subject to restrictions under Article 15(5) prescribed by law in the interests of national economy or in relation to professional, technical, academic, financial and other qualifications necessary for the practice of the occupation.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_255,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Under what circumstances may the constitutional right to freedom of movement be restricted?,"Article 15(6) provides that the exercise and operation of the freedom of movement (Article 14(1)(h)) may be restricted by law in the interests of national economy. The freedom to return to Sri Lanka (Article 14(1)(i)) is subject to the broader restrictions applicable under Article 15(7), which covers all fundamental rights and permits restrictions in the interests of national security, public order, and the protection of public health or morality.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_256,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can a citizen be permanently denied the right to return to Sri Lanka under the Constitution?,"Article 14(1)(i) recognises every citizen's freedom to return to Sri Lanka. Article 15(6) permits restrictions on the freedom of movement (Article 14(1)(h)) only in the interests of national economy, while Article 15(7) permits broader restrictions on all fundamental rights, including the freedom to return, in the interests of national security, public order, the protection of public health or morality, or to secure due recognition of the rights and freedoms of others; but all such restrictions must be prescribed by law. A permanent and absolute denial with no legal basis would be inconsistent with the constitutional guarantee.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_257,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional limitations apply to the freedom of association?,Article 15(4) provides that the freedom of association (Article 14(1)(c)) and the freedom to form and join a trade union (Article 14(1)(d)) shall be subject to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law in the interests of racial and religious harmony or in relation to the national economy.,"Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_258,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A trade union is banned citing economic reasons. Which constitutional provisions become relevant?,"Article 14(1)(d) guarantees the freedom to form and join a trade union. Article 15(4) permits restrictions on this freedom in the interests of, among other things, the national economy, provided the restriction is prescribed by law. Whether a blanket ban constitutes a permissible restriction is an interpretive question; the affected persons could invoke Article 126 to challenge whether the restriction is constitutionally justified.","Article 14, Article 15, Article 126","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_259,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,How does Article 15 attempt to balance individual liberty and national security?,"Article 15 sets out a closed list of grounds on which fundamental rights may be restricted: national security, public order, protection of public health or morality, racial and religious harmony, and respect for the rights of others. Restrictions must be prescribed by law. Article 15(8) provides that restrictions on rights in Chapter III apply to members of the armed forces and police only to the extent compatible with the requirements of those services, as provided by law.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_260,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can torture be justified by emergency circumstances under the Constitution?,"Article 11 declares that no person shall be subjected to torture or to cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment. Article 15(1) provides that the exercise of the rights declared in Articles 13(5) and 13(6) may be restricted in the interests of national security, but Article 11 is not included among the rights subject to restriction under Article 15. The prohibition on torture is therefore absolute and admits of no emergency exception under the Constitution.",Article 15,Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_261,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Which institutions are subject to the constitutional right of access to information?,"Article 14A(1) provides that every citizen has the right of access to information required for the exercise or protection of a citizen's right held by: (a) the State, a Ministry, Government Department or statutory body; (b) any Ministry or Department of a Province or any provincial statutory body; (c) any local authority; or (d) any other body established by law to perform a public function.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_262,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Does the right to information under Article 14A extend to private bodies?,"Article 14A applies specifically to information held by the State, Ministries, Government Departments, statutory bodies, provincial bodies, local authorities and bodies established by law to perform public functions. Private bodies not performing a public function are not within the scope of Article 14A, though Parliament may extend coverage by law.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_263,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What categories of institutions fall under the right to information framework in Article 14A?,"Article 14A(1) identifies four categories: (a) central government — the State, Ministries, Government Departments and statutory bodies; (b) provincial — Ministries, Departments and statutory bodies of provinces; (c) local authorities; and (d) any other body established by law to perform a public function. The right applies to information in these bodies that is required for the exercise or protection of a citizen's right.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_264,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can access to information be restricted to protect judicial impartiality?,Article 14A provides the right of access to information as provided for by law. The governing law enacted by Parliament may specify grounds on which access may be restricted. The Constitution itself does not enumerate the grounds in Article 14A; those are left to Parliament's law. Whether judicial impartiality can be a ground for restriction depends on the statutory framework implementing Article 14A.,Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_265,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A government ministry refuses to disclose information citing national security. What constitutional basis may support this?,"Article 14A grants the right of access to information as provided for by law. The constitutional provision envisages that Parliament may enact legislation governing the right, including permissible grounds for withholding information. National security is a common ground in such laws. The ministry's refusal would need to be based on a specific provision of the law enacted under Article 14A; a bare assertion of national security without statutory grounding would not suffice.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_266,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Does the Constitution recognise privacy concerns as grounds for restricting access to information?,"Article 14A grants the right of access to information as provided for by law, leaving it to Parliament to define the scope and any restrictions. Protection of privacy is commonly included among the grounds for restricting information access in implementing legislation, but the Constitution itself does not specify this in Article 14A.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_267,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can corporations be treated as 'citizens' for the purpose of Article 14A?,"Article 14A(1) grants the right of access to information to 'every citizen'. Under Sri Lankan constitutional law, corporations are not citizens; only natural persons who are citizens of Sri Lanka qualify. A corporation cannot independently invoke Article 14A, though its individual citizen-shareholders or directors may be able to do so if their own rights require the information.",Article 14A,"Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_268,Interpretive,Easy,Constitutional,A local authority denies a citizen's request for public records without explanation. Which constitutional provision may be invoked?,"Article 14A(1)(c) grants every citizen the right of access to information held by any local authority that is required for the exercise or protection of a citizen's right. An unexplained denial may constitute an infringement of this fundamental right, which can be challenged under Article 126 through the Supreme Court's fundamental rights jurisdiction.","Article 14A, Article 126","Art. 14A: (1) Every citizen shall have the right of Right of access to access to any information as provided for by law, being information information that is required for the exercise or protection of a citize || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_269,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,In what interests may freedom of speech and expression be restricted under the Constitution?,"Article 15(2) provides that freedom of speech and expression including publication (Article 14(1)(a)) may be restricted by law in the interests of racial and religious harmony or in relation to parliamentary privilege, contempt of court, defamation or incitement to an offence.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_270,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can the freedom of association be restricted in the interests of the national economy?,Article 15(4) expressly includes the national economy as one of the interests for which restrictions on the freedom of association (Article 14(1)(c)) and the freedom to form and join a trade union (Article 14(1)(d)) may be prescribed by law.,"Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_271,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What restrictions may be placed on the freedom of occupation under the Constitution?,"Article 15(5) provides that the freedom to engage in any lawful occupation, profession, trade, business or enterprise (Article 14(1)(g)) may be restricted by law in the interests of national economy or in relation to professional, technical, academic, financial and other qualifications necessary for practising any profession or carrying on any occupation, trade, business or enterprise.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (1) The exercise and operation of the fundamental fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles rights 13(5) and 13(6) shall be subject only to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law i",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_272,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can members of the Armed Forces have their fundamental rights restricted under the Constitution?,"Article 15(8) provides that the exercise and operation of the fundamental rights declared and recognised by Articles 12(1), 13 and 14 shall, in their application to the members of the Armed Forces, Police Force and other forces responsible for the maintenance of public order, be subject to such restrictions as may be prescribed by law in the interests of proper discharge of their duties and the maintenance of discipline among them.","Article 14, Article 15","Art. 14: (1) Every citizen is entitled to  speech, assembly, (a) the freedom of speech and expression association, including publication; occupation, movement, & c. (b) the freedom of peaceful assembly; (c) th || Art. 15: (8) The exercise and operation of the fundamental rights declared and recognized by Articles 12(1), 13 and 14 shall, in their application to the members of the Armed Forces, Police Force and o",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_273,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,In which languages may a Member of Parliament perform duties?,"Article 20 provides that a Member of Parliament, a member of a Provincial Council or a Local Authority shall be entitled to perform his duties and discharge his functions in Parliament, in such Provincial Council or Local Authority, in either of the National Languages, which are Sinhala and Tamil under Article 19.","Article 20, Article 19",Art. 20: A Member of Parliament or 6[a member of a Use of Provincial Council or a Local Authority] shall be entitled National Languages in to perform his duties and discharge his functions in Parliament Parlia || Art. 19: The National Languages of Sri Lanka shall be National Languages Sinhala and Tamil.,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_274,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can public examinations be conducted in either National Language?,"Article 20, read with Article 19, establishes that the National Languages are Sinhala and Tamil, and citizens are entitled to perform public duties in either National Language. Article 21 directly provides that every person is entitled to be educated through the medium of either of the National Languages.","Article 19, Article 20, Article 21",Art. 19: The National Languages of Sri Lanka shall be National Languages Sinhala and Tamil. || Art. 20: A Member of Parliament or 6[a member of a Use of Provincial Council or a Local Authority] shall be entitled National Languages in to perform his duties and discharge his functions in Parliament Parlia || Art. 21: (1) A person shall be entitled to be educated through the medium of either of the National Languages: Provided that the provisions of this paragraph shall not apply to an institution of higher education where the medium of instruction is a language other than a National Language.,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_275,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What language rights do citizens have when dealing with government public officials?,"Article 22 provides that Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages of administration throughout Sri Lanka. Citizens are entitled to receive correspondence and communications from government institutions in either of the two National Languages. The President may, by Order, require government institutions to use both National Languages where necessary.",Article 22,Art. 22: (1) Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages 8[Languages of Administration of administration throughout Sri Lanka and Sinhala shall be the language of administration and be used for the maintenance of,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_276,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A Tamil-speaking citizen in Colombo requests official documents in Tamil. What constitutional provisions apply?,"Article 22(1) provides that Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages of administration throughout Sri Lanka. A Tamil-speaking citizen is constitutionally entitled to receive official documents in Tamil. Under Article 22, the President may make Orders specifying that government institutions shall transact business in both National Languages, and citizens have language rights under Chapter IV enforceable through Article 126.","Article 22, Article 126",Art. 22: (1) Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages 8[Languages of Administration of administration throughout Sri Lanka and Sinhala shall be the language of administration and be used for the maintenance of || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_277,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Can a Member of Parliament speak in Tamil during parliamentary proceedings?,"Yes. Article 20 expressly provides that a Member of Parliament shall be entitled to perform his duties and discharge his functions in Parliament in either of the National Languages, which under Article 19 are Sinhala and Tamil.","Article 19, Article 20",Art. 19: The National Languages of Sri Lanka shall be National Languages Sinhala and Tamil. || Art. 20: A Member of Parliament or 6[a member of a Use of Provincial Council or a Local Authority] shall be entitled National Languages in to perform his duties and discharge his functions in Parliament Parlia,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_278,Factual,Hard,Constitutional,A Tamil-speaking citizen requests government services in Tamil in a predominantly Sinhala area. What rights apply?,Article 22(1) provides that Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages of administration throughout Sri Lanka. The right to receive government services in Tamil is not confined to the Northern and Eastern Provinces; it applies nationally. The President may issue Orders to ensure that government institutions transact business in both National Languages where necessary.,Article 22,Art. 22: (1) Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages 8[Languages of Administration of administration throughout Sri Lanka and Sinhala shall be the language of administration and be used for the maintenance of,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_279,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,How does the Constitution address language equality in public administration?,Article 22 establishes Sinhala and Tamil as languages of administration throughout Sri Lanka and requires government institutions to maintain public records and transact business in those languages. The President has power to make Orders specifying use of both National Languages in public institutions. Article 22 read with the broader Chapter IV language rights creates a framework for equal treatment of both language communities in administration.,Article 22,Art. 22: (1) Sinhala and Tamil shall be the languages 8[Languages of Administration of administration throughout Sri Lanka and Sinhala shall be the language of administration and be used for the maintenance of,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_280,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Does the Constitution distinguish between citizenship acquired by descent and by registration?,"Article 26(2) provides that a citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka, whether such person became entitled to citizenship by descent or by virtue of registration in accordance with the law relating to citizenship. The mode of acquisition is therefore constitutionally irrelevant to status.",Article 26,"Art. 26: (1) There shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. (2) A citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka,",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_281,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can a citizen be deprived of citizenship arbitrarily under the Constitution?,"Article 26(3) provides that no distinction shall be drawn and no disability or disadvantage shall be imposed, on the ground only that a citizen became entitled to citizenship by descent or by registration. This protects equal status among citizens. The Constitution itself does not address deprivation of citizenship, which is governed by the law relating to citizenship; however, any deprivation must comply with constitutional rights including equality under Article 12.","Article 26, Article 12","Art. 26: (1) There shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. (2) A citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka, || Art. 12: (1) All persons are equal before the law and are equality entitled to the equal protection of the law. (2) No citizen shall be discriminated against on the grounds of race, religion, language, caste,",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_282,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A person claims unequal treatment because they obtained citizenship through registration rather than descent. What constitutional principle applies?,Article 26(3) expressly provides that no distinction shall be drawn and no disability or disadvantage shall be imposed on a citizen on the ground only that the citizen became entitled to citizenship by registration rather than by descent. Treating such a person less favourably solely on that ground would violate Article 26(3) and could be challenged as a violation of fundamental rights.,Article 26,"Art. 26: (1) There shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. (2) A citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka,",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_283,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What protections exist for citizens acquired under earlier citizenship laws?,"Article 26(1) establishes one status of citizenship known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. Article 26(2) and (3) provide that this status applies to all citizens regardless of how citizenship was acquired — by descent or by registration — and prohibit any distinction, disability or disadvantage based solely on the mode of acquisition.",Article 26,"Art. 26: (1) There shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. (2) A citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka,",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_284,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can Parliament create multiple categories of citizenship under the Constitution?,Article 26(1) provides that there shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka. Article 26(2) and (3) reinforce this by prohibiting distinctions between citizens based on the mode of acquisition of citizenship. Parliament may regulate the conditions for acquiring citizenship but cannot constitutionally create a hierarchy of citizens with different rights.,Article 26,"Art. 26: (1) There shall be one status of citizenship of Sri Lanka known as the status of a citizen of Sri Lanka. (2) A citizen of Sri Lanka shall for all purposes be described only as a citizen of Sri Lanka,",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_285,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What social objectives are identified under Article 27(2) as Directive Principles?,Article 27(2) identifies a broad set of social objectives including: establishing a democratic socialist society founded on respect for human values; ensuring an adequate standard of living for citizens; eliminating economic and social privilege; equal distribution of the material resources of the community; eliminating discrimination; ensuring equal opportunity; and protecting the environment.,Article 27,"Art. 27: (1) The Directive Principles of State Policy Directive Principles herein contained shall guide Parliament, the President of State and the Cabinet of Ministers in the enactment of laws and Policy the g",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_286,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional provisions relate to an adequate standard of living?,"Article 27(2)(c) directs the State to ensure the realization by all citizens of an adequate standard of living for themselves and their families, including adequate food, clothing and housing and the continuous improvement of living conditions. Article 27(2)(e) further directs the State to ensure the equitable distribution among all citizens of the material resources of the community so as to subserve the common good. Article 27(2)(f) requires that the means of production, distribution and exchange are not concentrated and centralised in the State or in the hands of a privileged few. These Directive Principles guide government but are not directly justiciable under Article 29.",Article 27,"Art. 27: (2) The State is pledged to establish in Sri Lanka a Democratic Socialist Society, the objectives of which include  (a) the full realization of the fundamental rights and freedoms of all persons; (b)",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_287,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A citizen files a lawsuit demanding judicial enforcement of Article 27 Directive Principles. What constitutional issue arises?,Article 29 expressly provides that the provisions of Chapter VI (Directive Principles of State Policy and Fundamental Duties) do not confer or impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency with such provisions shall be raised in any court or tribunal. The citizen's claim would therefore fail because Article 29 makes the Directive Principles non-justiciable.,"Article 27, Article 29","Art. 27: (1) The Directive Principles of State Policy Directive Principles herein contained shall guide Parliament, the President of State and the Cabinet of Ministers in the enactment of laws and Policy the g || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_288,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,How does Article 27 address the goal of eliminating economic inequality?,"Article 27(2)(e) directs the State to ensure the equitable distribution among all citizens of the material resources of the community so as to subserve the common good. Article 27(2)(f) requires that the means of production, distribution and exchange are not concentrated and centralised in the State, State agencies or in the hands of a privileged few, but are dispersed among and owned by all the People. Article 27(7) provides that the State shall eliminate economic and social privilege and disparity. Article 27(8) provides that the State shall ensure that the operation of the economic system does not result in the concentration of wealth and the means of production to the common detriment. These Directive Principles are not justiciable under Article 29 but guide all organs of government.","Article 27, Article 27","Art. 27: (2) The State is pledged to establish in Sri Lanka a Democratic Socialist Society, the objectives of which include  (a) the full realization of the fundamental rights and freedoms of all persons; (b) || Art. 27: (3) The State shall safeguard the independence, sovereignty, unity and the territorial integrity of Sri Lanka. (4) The State shall strengthen and broaden the democratic structure of government and the",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_289,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional protections exist for children and youth under the Directive Principles?,"Article 27(13) directs the State to promote with special care the interests of children and youth, so as to ensure their full development, physical, mental, moral, religious and social. This is a Directive Principle that guides the governance of Sri Lanka but is not directly enforceable in a court under Article 29.","Article 27, Article 29","Art. 27: (11) The State shall create the necessary economic and social environment to enable people of all religious faiths to make a reality of their religious principles. (12) The State shall recognize and protect the family as the basic unit of society. (13) The State shall promote with special care the interests of children and youth, so as to ensure their full development, physical, mental, moral, religious and social. || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
