QAC_291,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A citizen intentionally damages state property during a protest. Which constitutional duty may be relevant?,"Article 28(d) imposes a fundamental duty on every person in Sri Lanka to protect public property and to combat misuse and waste of public property. Intentional damage to state property during a protest would be inconsistent with this constitutional duty. However, under Article 29, these duties are not directly enforceable as legal obligations in court; they are constitutional principles.","Article 28, Article 29",Art. 28: The exercise and enjoyment of rights and Fundamental freedoms are inseparable from the performance of duties duties and obligations and accordingly it is the duty of every person in Sri Lanka  (a) to || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_292,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,What duty of environmental conservation does the Constitution impose on citizens?,"Article 28(f) provides that it is the duty of every person in Sri Lanka to protect nature and conserve its riches. This is one of the fundamental duties in Article 28, though Article 29 provides that the fundamental duties are not enforceable in any court or tribunal.","Article 28, Article 29",Art. 28: The exercise and enjoyment of rights and Fundamental freedoms are inseparable from the performance of duties duties and obligations and accordingly it is the duty of every person in Sri Lanka  (a) to || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_293,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can courts directly enforce Fundamental Duties against citizens under the Constitution?,Article 29 expressly provides that the provisions of Chapter VI — which includes both the Directive Principles of State Policy and the Fundamental Duties under Article 28 — do not confer or impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable in any court or tribunal. Courts therefore cannot directly impose sanctions on citizens for failure to perform constitutional duties.,"Article 28, Article 29",Art. 28: The exercise and enjoyment of rights and Fundamental freedoms are inseparable from the performance of duties duties and obligations and accordingly it is the duty of every person in Sri Lanka  (a) to || Art. 29: The provisions of this Chapter do not confer or Principles of State impose legal rights or obligations and are not enforceable Policy and in any court or tribunal. No question of inconsistency fundame,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
