QAC_302,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,What constitutional checks exist on the powers of the President?,"The Constitution provides several checks on presidential power: Parliament exercises legislative power and controls public finance (Articles 75, 148); the President's legislative actions are subject to the Supreme Court's constitutional jurisdiction (Article 120); fundamental rights infringements by executive action may be challenged in the Supreme Court (Article 126); the President is responsible to Parliament (Article 42); and Cabinet decisions require collective responsibility.","Article 75, Article 126, Article 148","Art. 75: Parliament shall have power to make laws, Legislative power including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Const || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut || Art. 148: Parliament shall have full control over public Control of finance. No tax, rate or any other levy shall be imposed by Parliament over public any local authority or any other public authority, except f",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_303,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What forms of pardon may the President grant under Article 34?,Article 34(1) provides that the President may grant: (a) a free or conditional pardon; (b) a respite of the execution of any sentence; (c) a substitution of a less severe punishment for any punishment imposed; or (d) a remission of the whole or any part of any punishment imposed or of any penalty or forfeiture otherwise due to the Republic.,Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_304,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Can the President substitute a lesser punishment for a convicted offender?,Yes. Article 34(1)(c) expressly provides that the President may substitute a less severe form of punishment for any punishment imposed on an offender.,Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_305,Procedural,Hard,Constitutional,What procedure applies before the President grants a pardon to a person sentenced to death?,"Article 34(1) provides that the President's pardon power applies to any offender convicted in any court in Sri Lanka. However, before exercising the pardon power in relation to a person condemned to death, the President shall cause a report to be made by the Judge who tried the case, forward that report to the Attorney-General for advice, and then have the report with the Attorney-General's advice sent to the Minister in charge of the subject of Justice, who shall forward it with his recommendation to the President.",Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_306,Interpretive,Easy,Constitutional,A convicted offender seeks remission of punishment. Which constitutional provision applies?,Article 34(1)(d) provides that the President may remit the whole or any part of any punishment imposed on an offender or of any penalty or forfeiture otherwise due to the Republic. An offender seeking remission should petition the President for exercise of this prerogative of mercy.,Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_307,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can presidential pardons affect electoral disqualifications?,"Article 34(2) provides that the President may in the case of any person who has become subject to any disqualification specified in the Constitution by reason of a conviction, on the advice of the Cabinet of Ministers, grant a pardon suspending the operation of such disqualification. This specifically addresses the lifting of electoral disqualifications arising from conviction.",Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_308,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can accomplices receive pardons in exchange for information under the Constitution?,"Article 34(3) provides that the President may, in the case of any person charged with an offence, grant a pardon on condition that such person gives evidence for the prosecution. This constitutional provision allows accomplice evidence arrangements.",Article 34,"Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_309,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can presidential pardon powers override judicial convictions entirely?,"Article 34(1) allows the President to grant a free pardon, which would effectively wipe out the conviction and its consequences. However, the pardon power is an executive prerogative of mercy and does not invalidate the court's judgment; it merely removes the legal consequences. The Supreme Court's jurisdiction is not displaced and any procedural challenge to the pardon itself could be raised under Article 126.","Article 34, Article 126","Art. 34: (1) The President may in the case of any Pardon offender convicted of any offence in any court within the Republic of Sri Lanka  (a) grant a pardon, either free or subject to lawful conditions; (b) gr || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_310,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can fundamental rights applications be filed regarding presidential actions?,"Article 35(1) provides that while a person holds office as President, no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against him in any court. However, the proviso expressly states that nothing in Article 35(1) shall restrict the right of any person to make an application under Article 126 in respect of infringement of a fundamental right.","Article 35, Article 126","Art. 35: (1) While any person holds office as President, Immunity of President no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against from suit him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omit || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_311,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,A citizen attempts to sue the sitting President personally. What constitutional barrier exists?,"Article 35(1) provides that while any person holds office as President, no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omitted, whether in his official or private capacity. This immunity persists for the duration of the Presidency. After leaving office, proceedings may be instituted subject to limitation periods.",Article 35,"Art. 35: (1) While any person holds office as President, Immunity of President no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against from suit him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omit",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_312,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Does presidential immunity under Article 35 apply to acts done in a private capacity?,Yes. Article 35(1) expressly covers acts or omissions by the President in both his official and his private capacity. The immunity is personal and temporal — it runs for the duration of office — not limited to official acts.,Article 35,"Art. 35: (1) While any person holds office as President, Immunity of President no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against from suit him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omit",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_313,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can limitation periods for legal proceedings be suspended during the period a person holds the Presidency?,"Article 35(2) provides that where provision is made by law limiting the time within which proceedings may be brought against any person, the period during which that person holds the office of President shall not be taken into account in calculating the period of limitation. Time-limits are thus effectively paused for the duration of the Presidency.",Article 35,"Art. 35: (1) While any person holds office as President, Immunity of President no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against from suit him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omit",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_314,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can the Supreme Court review all exercises of presidential power?,"The Supreme Court has jurisdiction over fundamental rights violations by executive or administrative action (Article 126) and constitutional jurisdiction over Bills (Article 120). However, Article 35(1) prevents proceedings against the President personally. Article 41J limits review of Constitutional Council decisions. The scope of judicial review of presidential acts depends on the specific power in question and whether a justiciable constitutional right is engaged.","Article 35, Article 126, Article 120","Art. 35: (1) While any person holds office as President, Immunity of President no proceedings shall be instituted or continued against from suit him in any court or tribunal in respect of anything done or omit || Art. 126: (1) The Supreme Court shall have sole and rights exclusive jurisdiction to hear and determine any question jurisdiction and its relating to the infringement or imminent infringement by exercise execut || Art. 120: The Supreme Court shall have sole and Constitutional exclusive jurisdiction to determine any question as to Jurisdiction of the Supreme whether any Bill or any provision thereof is inconsistent Court",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_315,Procedural,Medium,Constitutional,A citizen challenges the constitutionality of a Bill before Parliament enacts it. Which constitutional procedure applies?,"Article 120 provides that the Supreme Court has sole and exclusive jurisdiction to determine whether any Bill or any provision thereof is inconsistent with the Constitution. Under Article 121, a citizen may petition the Supreme Court within the specified time after the Bill is placed on the Order Paper of Parliament. If the Supreme Court determines a Bill is inconsistent with the Constitution, the Bill may not become law unless passed by the required special majority or approved at a Referendum.",Article 120,Art. 120: The Supreme Court shall have sole and Constitutional exclusive jurisdiction to determine any question as to Jurisdiction of the Supreme whether any Bill or any provision thereof is inconsistent Court,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_316,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,Can enacted laws be challenged for constitutional validity after the Speaker has certified them?,"Article 80(1) provides that a Bill becomes law upon the endorsement of the Speaker's certificate. Article 80(3) provides that no court or tribunal shall inquire into, pronounce upon or in any manner call in question the validity of any Act on any ground whatsoever after the Speaker has certified it. The Speaker's certificate is therefore conclusive as to constitutional validity.",Article 80,"Art. 80: (1) Subject to the provisions of paragraph (2) of becomes law this Article, a Bill passed by Parliament shall become law when the certificate of the Speaker is endorsed thereon. (2) Where the Cabinet",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_317,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional protections support the independence of the judiciary?,"Article 107 provides that the Chief Justice, the President of the Court of Appeal and every other judge of the Supreme Court and the Court of Appeal shall be appointed by the President subject to the approval of the Constitutional Council, by warrant under his hand. Every such judge holds office during good behaviour and may only be removed by an order of the President made after an address of Parliament supported by a majority of the total number of Members of Parliament. Their salaries are charged on the Consolidated Fund (Article 150) and may not be reduced during service, ensuring financial independence.","Article 107, Article 150","Art. 107: (1) The Chief Justice, the President of the Court of Appeal and every other judge of the Supreme Court and the Court of Appeal shall be appointed by the President subject to the approval of the Constitutional Council, by warrant under his hand. (2) Every such Judge shall hold office during good behaviour and shall not be removed except by an order of the President made after an address of Parliament supported by a majority of the total number of Members of Parliament (including those not present) has been presented to the President for such removal on the ground of proved misbehaviour or incapacity. || Art. 150: (1) Save as otherwise expressly provided in Withdrawal paragraphs (3) and (4) of this Article, no sum shall be of sums from Consolidated withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under the Fund auth",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_318,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,How does the Constitution prevent interference with the judiciary and judicial officers?,"Article 111C (renumbered from former Article 116 by the Seventeenth Amendment) provides that every person who, without legal authority, interferes or attempts to interfere with the exercise or performance of the judicial powers or functions of any judge, presiding officer, or public officer shall be guilty of an offence punishable by the High Court with imprisonment of up to one year, or a fine, or both. Upon conviction the offender may additionally be disqualified from being an elector or holding any public office for a period not exceeding seven years. This provision gives direct constitutional backing to the protection of judicial independence.",Article 111C,"Art. 111C: (2) Every person who, without legal authority, interferes or attempts to interfere with the exercise or performance of the judicial powers or functions of any judge, presiding officer, public officer",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_319,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,What constitutional control does Parliament exercise over public finance?,"Article 148 provides that Parliament shall have full control over public finance. No tax, rate or any other levy shall be imposed by any local authority or public authority except by or under the authority of a law passed by Parliament or of any existing law.",Article 148,"Art. 148: Parliament shall have full control over public Control of finance. No tax, rate or any other levy shall be imposed by Parliament over public any local authority or any other public authority, except f",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_320,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can money be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund without constitutional authority?,Article 150(1) provides that no sum shall be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under the authority of a warrant under the hand of the Minister in charge of Finance. Article 150(2) provides that no such warrant shall be issued unless the sum has been granted for specified public services by resolution of Parliament or by law. Unauthorised withdrawal is thus constitutionally impermissible.,Article 150,"Art. 150: (1) Save as otherwise expressly provided in Withdrawal paragraphs (3) and (4) of this Article, no sum shall be of sums from Consolidated withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under the Fund auth",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_321,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,Can Parliament enact laws affecting public revenue without any special procedure?,"Article 152 provides that no Bill or motion authorising disposal of or imposing charges on the Consolidated Fund or other public funds, or imposing, repealing, augmenting or reducing any tax, shall be introduced in Parliament except by a Minister, and unless the Bill or motion has been approved by the Cabinet of Ministers or in such manner as the Cabinet may authorise. Such Bills therefore require Cabinet approval before introduction.",Article 152,"Art. 152: No Bill or motion, authorizing the disposal of, or provisions the imposition of charges upon, the Consolidated Fund or as to Bills other funds of the Republic, or the imposition of any tax or affectin",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_322,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional role does the Board of Ministers perform within a Province?,Article 154B provides that there shall be a Governor for each Province. The executive power in the Province with respect to matters on which the Provincial Council has power to make statutes is exercised by the Governor on the advice of the Board of Ministers of the Province (Article 154C). The Board of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Provincial Council.,"Article 154B, Article 154C","Art. 154B: (1) There shall be a Governor for each Governor Province for which a Provincial Council has been established in accordance with Article || Art. 154C: Executive power extending to the matters with respect to which a Provincial Council has power to make statutes shall be exercised by the Governor of the Province for which that Provincial Council is established, either directly or through Ministers of the Board of Ministers, or through officers subordinate to him, in accordance with Article 154F.",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_323,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,A Provincial Council enacts a statute that conflicts with national law. What constitutional issue arises?,"Article 154G provides that a Provincial Council may make statutes with respect to matters in the Provincial Council List (List I of the Ninth Schedule). Under Article 154G(5), if a Provincial Council statute is inconsistent with a law enacted by Parliament, the Parliament law shall prevail and the Provincial Council statute shall, to the extent of the inconsistency, be void. Parliament's legislative supremacy over concurrent and reserved matters is preserved.","Article 154G, Article 75","Art. 154G: (1) Every Provincial Council may, subject to the Statutes of Provincial provisions of the Constitution, make statutes applicable to Councils the Province for which it is established, with respect to a || Art. 75: Parliament shall have power to make laws, Legislative power including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Const",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_324,Interpretive,Hard,Constitutional,How does the Constitution distribute powers between the centre and the provinces?,"The Constitution establishes three legislative lists: the Reserved List (List II, Parliament only), the Provincial Council List (List I, provincial statutes), and the Concurrent List (List III, both). Article 154G gives Provincial Councils power to legislate on matters in List I. On concurrent matters, Parliament's law prevails in case of inconsistency. Executive power follows legislative competence under Articles 154B–154C.","Article 154G, Article 75","Art. 154G: (1) Every Provincial Council may, subject to the Statutes of Provincial provisions of the Constitution, make statutes applicable to Councils the Province for which it is established, with respect to a || Art. 75: Parliament shall have power to make laws, Legislative power including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Const",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_325,Factual,Hard,Constitutional,What constitutional powers apply when essential services are threatened in a Province?,"Article 154J provides that upon the making of a proclamation under the Public Security Ordinance on the ground that essential supplies and services are threatened or the security of Sri Lanka is threatened, the President may give directions to any Governor as to the manner in which executive power with respect to matters in the Provincial Council List shall be exercised.",Article 154J,"Art. 154J: (1) Upon the making of a proclamation Public Security under the Public Security Ordinance or the law for the time being in force relating to public security, bringing the provisions of such Ordinance",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_326,Factual,Hard,Constitutional,What powers does the President have when a Provincial Council fails to comply with constitutional directions?,"Article 154K provides that where the Governor or any Provincial Council has failed to comply with directions given under the Constitution, it shall be lawful for the President to hold that a situation has arisen in which the administration of the Province cannot be carried on in accordance with the constitutional provisions. The President may then take such measures as are authorised by law for the governance of the Province.",Article 154K,"Art. 154K: Where the Governor or any Provincial comply with directions Council has failed to comply with, or give effect to any directions given to such Governor or such Council under this Chapter of the Constit",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_327,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,Which constitutional body oversees elections in Sri Lanka?,"Under Articles 103 and 104A–104B (as amended), the Elections Commission is the constitutional body responsible for superintending, directing and controlling the preparation of electoral registers and the conduct of elections and referendums. The Commission exercises all powers previously vested in the Commissioner-General of Elections.",Article 104B,"Art. 104B: (1) The Commission shall exercise, perform Powers, functions and and discharge all such powers, duties and functions duties of the conferred or imposed on or assigned to  Commission (a) the Commission",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_328,Factual,Easy,Constitutional,A public officer stands as a candidate during an election period. What constitutional restriction applies?,"Article 102 provides that when a public officer or an officer of a public corporation is a candidate at any election, he shall be deemed to be on leave from the date he stands nominated until the conclusion of the election. During this period he shall not exercise, perform or discharge any of the powers, duties or functions of his office.",Article 102,"Art. 102: When a public officer or an officer of a public Public officer corporation is a candidate at any election, he shall be or an officer of a public deemed to be on leave from the date on which he stands",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_329,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,How does the Constitution attempt to ensure electoral fairness?,"The Constitution establishes the Elections Commission (Article 104B) with powers to direct and control election conduct. Article 104C requires the police to be made available to the Commission during elections. Article 88 sets elector qualifications, and Articles 89–91 specify disqualifications for electors and Members of Parliament. Articles 104A provide that Commission decisions are final subject to specified judicial review.","Article 104B, Article 102","Art. 104B: (1) The Commission shall exercise, perform Powers, functions and and discharge all such powers, duties and functions duties of the conferred or imposed on or assigned to  Commission (a) the Commission || Art. 102: When a public officer or an officer of a public Public officer corporation is a candidate at any election, he shall be or an officer of a public deemed to be on leave from the date on which he stands",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_330,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What constitutional remedies exist for a disputed election?,"For elections to the membership of Parliament, Article 144 provides that the Court of Appeal has jurisdiction to try parliamentary election petitions in terms of any law applicable in that behalf. For presidential elections and referendums, Article 130 confers jurisdiction on the Supreme Court to hear and determine any legal proceeding relating to the election of the President or the validity of a referendum. The Supreme Court under Article 130 also hears appeals from orders or judgments of the Court of Appeal in election petition cases.","Article 144, Article 130",Art. 144: The Court of Appeal shall have and exercise Parliamentary jurisdiction to try election petitions in respect of the elect || Art. 130: The Supreme Court shall have the power to hear and determine and make such orders as provided for by law on (a) any lega,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_331,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can Parliament exercise legislative power without a referendum in all situations?,"Article 75 grants Parliament power to make laws including amending the Constitution. However, Article 83 provides that a Bill to amend or which is inconsistent with certain entrenched provisions (including Articles 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11) must be passed by a two-thirds majority of Parliament and approved at a Referendum. Parliament therefore cannot always exercise legislative power without a referendum.","Article 75, Article 83","Art. 75: Parliament shall have power to make laws, Legislative power including laws having retrospective effect and repealing or amending any provision of the Constitution, or adding any provision to the Const || Art. 83: Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the of certain provisions of Article 82  Bills at a Referendum (a) a Bill for the amendment or for the repeal and replacement of or which is inconsistent wi",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_332,Interpretive,Medium,Constitutional,Can Parliament amend every constitutional provision without public approval?,"No. Article 83 requires that Bills amending or inconsistent with entrenched provisions (Articles 1, 2, 3, 6–11, Article 83 itself, and certain electoral and term-extension provisions) must be both passed by a two-thirds majority in Parliament and approved at a Referendum. Ordinary constitutional amendments under Article 82 require only a two-thirds parliamentary majority.","Article 82, Article 83","Art. 82: (5) A Bill for the amendment of any provision of the Constitution or for the repeal and replacement of the Constitution, shall become law if the number of votes cast in favour thereof amounts to not l || Art. 83: Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the of certain provisions of Article 82  Bills at a Referendum (a) a Bill for the amendment or for the repeal and replacement of or which is inconsistent wi",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_333,Factual,Medium,Constitutional,What is the constitutional purpose of holding a Referendum?,"Article 85 provides that certain Bills and any Bill that Parliament certifies for submission to the People by Referendum must receive the approval of the People. Article 83 makes a Referendum mandatory for Bills amending entrenched constitutional provisions. Article 86 additionally permits the President to submit any matter of national importance to the People by Referendum, enabling direct popular participation in major constitutional decisions.","Article 83, Article 85, Article 86","Art. 83: Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the of certain provisions of Article 82  Bills at a Referendum (a) a Bill for the amendment or for the repeal and replacement of or which is inconsistent wi || Art. 85: (1) The President shall submit to the People by of Bills to Referendum every Bill or any provision in any Bill people by Referendum which the Cabinet of Ministers has certified as being intended to be || Art. 86: The President may, subject to the provisions of of matters of Article 85, submit to the People by Referendum any matter national importance to which in the opinion of the President is of national peop",AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
QAC_334,Procedural,Hard,Constitutional,A Bill seeks to alter the provisions on the unitary status of Sri Lanka. What additional constitutional procedure applies?,Article 2 declares Sri Lanka to be a Unitary State. Article 83(a) provides that a Bill for the amendment of or which is inconsistent with Article 2 requires not only a two-thirds majority of all Members of Parliament but also approval at a Referendum. This double-lock procedure applies because Article 2 is among the most entrenched provisions of the Constitution.,"Article 83, Article 85",Art. 83: Notwithstanding anything to the contrary in the of certain provisions of Article 82  Bills at a Referendum (a) a Bill for the amendment or for the repeal and replacement of or which is inconsistent wi || Art. 85: (1) The President shall submit to the People by of Bills to Referendum every Bill or any provision in any Bill people by Referendum which the Cabinet of Ministers has certified as being intended to be,AI-Generated (Unreviewed),2026-05-18,,,,,,,
